#!/usr/bin/env perl
use 5.016;
use strict;
use warnings;
use App::AnteREPL ();

my $status = eval { App::AnteREPL::main(@ARGV) };
if ($@) { print STDERR $@; exit 1; }
exit $status;

__END__

=head1 NAME

anterepl - edit and run Perl in a scrolling BASIC-style screen

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    anterepl
    anterepl program.pl
    anterepl --plain program.bperl
    anterepl --help

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Starts L<App::AnteREPL>. All REPL commands and subcommands must be uppercase,
including C<LIST>, C<AUTO OFF>, C<DUMP>, and C<PEEK>. Lowercase/mixed-case input
is ordinary Perl, executed immediately or stored during AUTO.
Type HELP for commands. LOAD reads ordinary Perl or
numbered programs; AUTO stores unnumbered input with an increment of 5.
AUTO OFF or Ctrl-C returns to immediate mode. EDIT opens the in-memory
program in your local editor, and EDIT ENV edits the current environment.
QUIT or EXIT returns to the shell on a fresh line; append C<!> to discard
unsaved source.

The visible terminal is the whole working screen ("VRAM"). Shrinking crops
rows and columns, warns, and keeps one inactive copy. Growing adds empty cells.
Enlarge to the size in the warning and use RECOVER to restore the copy;
restoration is never automatic. CLS clears the screen and its recovery copy.
The numbered program and live Perl values are unaffected by screen resizing.

CD and CHDIR change the session directory; lowercase C<chdir> remains Perl.
A mistaken C<cls> bareword receives an uppercase spelling hint. Valid keywords
and function calls retain Perl semantics, including C<say triple 14> and
prototype-based calls. Ctrl-C cancels or interrupts, then explains how to
leave with QUIT, QUIT!, or Ctrl-D.

See L<App::AnteREPL> for the full command reference and screen controls.

